<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<worksheet xml:id="ws_4_2" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
<title>Practice Worksheet</title>

<exercise>
<introduction>
<p>
Simplify using exponent rules. Write your answer in the form <m>kx^p</m> if possible.
</p>
</introduction>

<task workspace="1in">
<statement>
<p>
<m>y=5x^6\cdot4x^2</m>
</p>
</statement>
<solution>
<p>
<md>
y \quad = \quad 20x^{6+2} \quad = \quad 20x^8.
</md>
</p>
</solution>
</task>


<task workspace="1in">
<statement>
<p>
<m>y=\dfrac{1}{2}\sqrt[3]{x}</m>
</p>
</statement>
<solution>
<p>
<md>
y \quad = \quad \frac{1}{2} x^{1 /3}.
</md>
</p>
</solution>
</task>


<task workspace="1in">
<statement>
<p>
<m>y=\dfrac{\pi}{x}</m>
</p>
</statement>
<solution>
<p>
<md>
y \quad = \quad \pi  x^{-1}.
</md>
</p>
</solution>
</task>


<task workspace="1in">
<statement>
<p>
<m>y=(x^2)^4</m>
</p>
</statement>
<solution>
<p>
<md>
y \quad=\quad x^{2\cdot 4} \quad = \quad x^8.
</md>
</p>
</solution>
</task>


<task workspace="1in">
<statement>
<p>
<m>y=(0.7)^x</m>
</p>
</statement>
<solution>
<p>
Cannot be simplified further.
</p>
</solution>
</task>


<task workspace="1in">
<statement>
<p>
<m>y=(0.5x^6)^{-3}</m>
</p>
</statement>
<solution>
<p>
<md>
y \quad = \quad (0.5)^{-3}\cdot x^{6(-3)}\quad = \quad 8 \cdot x^{-18}.
</md>
</p>
</solution>
</task>


<task workspace="1in">
<statement>
<p>
<m>y=\dfrac{14x^{-2}}{21\sqrt{x^7}}</m>
</p>
</statement>
<solution>
<p>
Notice that <m>\frac{14}{21}</m> reduces to <m>\frac{2}{3}</m>. And, in the last step below we used that <m>-2-\frac{7}{2} = -\frac{4}{2}-\frac{7}{2}=-\frac{11}{2}</m> (simply subtracting fractions).
<md>
y \quad = \quad \frac{2 x^{-2}}{3x^{7 /2}}  \quad = \quad \frac{2}{3} x^{-2-7 /2} \quad = \quad \frac{2}{3}x^{-11 /2}.
</md>
</p>
</solution>
</task>


<task workspace="1in">
<statement>
<p>
<m>y=\dfrac{x^5+1}{x^5-1}</m>
</p>
</statement>
<solution>
<p>
This cannot be simplified any further using exponent rules.
</p>
</solution>
</task>


</exercise>



<exercise>
<introduction>
<p>
Solve each equation. When there is no real solution, state that to be the case.
</p>
</introduction>

<task workspace="1in">
<statement>
<p>
<m>a^{1/3}-3=5</m>
</p>
</statement>
<solution>
<p>
Adding <m>3</m> to both sides, <m>a^{1 /3} = 8</m>. Cubing each side (raise to the third power), then
<md>
\pbr{a^{1 /3}} ^3 = 8^3 \quad \implies \quad a = 8^3 = 512.
</md>
</p>
</solution>
</task>


<task workspace="1in">
<statement>
<p>
<m>1+\sqrt{2x+3}=6</m>
</p>
</statement>
<solution>
<p>
Subtracting <m>1</m>, then <m>\sqrt{2x+3}  = 5</m>. Squaring both sides,
<md>
\pbr{\sqrt{2x+3}}^2 = 5^2 \quad \implies \quad 2x+3 = 25 \quad \implies \quad 2x=22 \quad \implies \quad x=11 .
</md>
</p>
</solution>
</task>


<task workspace="1in">
<statement>
<p>
<m>7(b+2)^3-26=30</m>
</p>
</statement>
<solution>
<p>
Adding <m>26</m> to both sides, then <m>7(b+2)^3 = 56</m>. Divide both sides to <m>7</m> to get <m>(b+2)^3 = 8</m>. Then, we take the cube root:
<md>
\sqrt[3]{(b+2)^3} = \sqrt[3]{8} \quad \implies \quad b+2 = 2 \quad \implies \quad b=0  .
</md>
</p>
</solution>
</task>


<task workspace="1in">
<statement>
<p>
<m>2y^6+8=0</m>
</p>
</statement>
<solution>
<p>
Subtracting <m>8</m> from both sides, then <m>2y^6 = -8</m>. Dividing by <m>2</m>, then <m>y^6 = -4</m>. Note that this equation does not have a solution because we cannot take an even root (namely, the <m>6</m>th root) of a negative number.
</p>
</solution>
</task>


<task workspace="1in">
<statement>
<p>
<m>80=100+10x^5</m>
</p>
</statement>
<solution>
<p>
Subtracting <m>100</m>, then <m>-20 = 10x^5</m>. Dividing by <m>10</m> yields <m>-2 = x^5</m>. We <em>can</em> take the <m>5</m>th root here, <m>5</m> being an odd number. So
<md>
\sqrt[5]{-2} = \sqrt[5]{x^5} \quad \implies \quad \sqrt[5]{-2} = x   .
</md>
</p>
</solution>
</task>


<task>
<statement>
<p>
<m>(3w-5)^4+4=20</m>
</p>
</statement>
<solution>
<p>
Subtracting <m>4</m>, then <m>(3w-5)^4 = 16</m>. Taking the <m>4</m>th root of both sides yields
<md>
3w-5 = \pm \sqrt[4]{16} \quad \implies \quad 3w-5=\pm 2.
</md> One solution is when <m>3w-5=2</m>, which means <m>3w=7</m> so <m>w=\frac{7}{3}</m>. The second solution is when <m>3w-5=-2</m>, which means <m>3w=3</m> so <m>w=1</m>.

Hence the two solutions are <m>w=\frac{7}{3}</m> and <m>w=1</m>.
</p>
</solution>
</task>


</exercise>

</worksheet>